igious beliefs, so my poorly recited sutras might be disturbing to him!\" [2636] \"Can I listen too?\" Akane asked from the far end. [2637] Hokusai looked surprised as he lifted his head and looked at Onizuka's former fianc\u00e9e. [2638] \"Can I?\" [2639] \"Uh... Yes. Of course. If anyone else wants to listen, please come to the music room.\" [2640] \"We'll go too. Husband?\" [2641] \"Yeah.\" [2642] The couple conversed as usual, with Kikyo leading the way and Yoshino following. Jing Shen and I, along with Yuki, Uzuki, and Koto, also asked Yoshino to let us attend the concert. [2643] \"Do you want to go too, Zhi Du?\" asked Zhi Du, the silent poet with half his body leaning forward. [2644] Zhi Du picked his teeth with his little finger, \"It'll be too crowded in that room. Thanks, but no thanks!\" [2645] \"You really are peculiar,\" said Yoshino, glaring at Zhi Du. The candlelight made his cheeks look like they had been shaved off. As he narrowed his eyes, a chill ran down my spine. But Zhi Du didn't even look at Yoshino and continued to clean his teeth. [2646] \"Just bring the chairs in, and there's room for ten people. I'd like to start at 9:00. Is that okay for everyone?\" The pianist's expression softened, and he asked the crowd. [2647] \"Hmm, fine,\" Kiku placed her hand on her knee. \"Thank you, Higashiyama.\" [2648] \"You're welcome,\" Higashiyama replied with a tight-lipped smile, glancing at the clock on the wall. [2649] Eight o'clock. [2650] The pendulum swung, moving the shadow around. [2651] *\n[2652] I looked at my watch and realized it was 10 o'clock. [2653] Exhausted, I leaned back in my chair, feeling drained. [2654] \"Hokutsumi's performance was truly remarkable!\" Jang Godai spoke as if in a monologue. He, like me, was deeply immersed in the chair by the window, gazing at the garden outside. His face, though calm, showed signs of fatigue, and it was reflected in the candlelight on the piano lid. [2655] \"Yes, it seems that Matsukaze-san is very happy, and I'm relieved too. The encore of 'Parting Song' was great. Saying it was an encore seems too casual, but it was like a special performance just for us.\" [2656] \"Perhaps they would have performed an encore if the audience was different. It's not often that people from the village gather here to listen to him play.\" [2657] \"Yes, but there was once last month when Matsuzaki-san performed his own composition. Like today, many people expressed their desire to hear it, and everyone gathered here. It was a very intense piece, almost entirely in forte from beginning to end.\" [2658] \"Was Zhu Dejing there?\" [2659] \"Yes. He was the only one who didn't clap when it ended.\" [2660] \"An eccentric poet?\" [2661] The door creaked open, and we saw the eccentric poet standing at the entrance. [2662] \"What's going on? The requiem concert is over.\" [2663] \"That's why I came,\" Tetsudo said, entering and closing the door. His eyes glinted in the dim room. He stopped at the piano, extending his unshaven chin, scratching his beard occasionally. [2664] \"I came to play the piano. Even after listening to Takamizawa's performance, I couldn't give a good review. I may not play well, but I don't like that guy's Beethoven.\" [2665] Tetsudo faced the piano again, sitting down in the chair, and then opening the lid. Before I could ask what he was going to play, his fingers fell on the keys. It was the third movement of Beethoven's \"Storm.\" I remembered hearing him play it at some point. Why did he suddenly start from the third movement? I understood the reason. Everyone who likes the piano wants to play this piece. Seeing me sit up straight, Jinguji-sensei also straightened his back and focused his gaze on the performer. Tetsudo's playing was somewhat hasty, often roughly operating the pedals and making mistakes. Following Takamizawa's performance, there was indeed a gap. However, the sounds he produced had a vivid presence, as if they were sounds directly emitted by humans. I closed my eyes and listened attentively to the music halfway through the performance. [2666] \"Hmm, it was a thrilling performance.\" [2667] As soon as the performance ended, both of us, the audience, applauded enthusiastically. Zhi Du remained hunched over the piano, responding with a raised fist. [2668] \"Zhi Du! Encore!\" [2669] The poet rubbed his hands again and slowly placed his elongated fingers on the keyboard. A lazy, whimsical melody flowed out gently. He murmured:\n[2670] \"Lean out your window\"\n[2671] Golden Hair\n[2672] I heard you singing\n[2673] In the midnight air\n[2674] My book is closed\n[2675] I read no more\n[2676] Watching the fire-dance\n[2677] On the floor\n[2678] This is a song I've never heard before. The melody is dreamy yet beautiful. The lyrics are not difficult, so I try to understand them. [2679] I put down my book and leave the room\n[2680] Because I hear your voice coming from the other end of the darkness. [2681] You sing happily over and over again\n[2682] Your golden hair leans against the window\n[2683] After the short song ends, Zhu Du lifts his head. [2684] \"Is this your poem?\" I ask. [2685] Zhu Du says \"No\" and turns to us. [2686] \"It is Joyce (Note: Joyce, (James Joyce), 1882~1941, Irish, is one of the important writers of modernism, whose works are mostly narrated in the background of his homeland, Ireland, and is famous for his works such as Dubliners and Ulysses.) His famous works include \"Dubliners\", \"Ulysses\", and \"Goiden Hair\". [2687] I had never heard of James Joyce's poetry before. [2688] \"The song was written by Syd Barrett.\" Zhi Du said cheerfully. [2689] The voice of Jiang Sheng suddenly interrupted. \"Is it Syd Barrett?\" [2690] An unfamiliar name. [2691] Shido happily spread a smile. \"Do you know this song?\" [2692] \"I really like this song and I really like Syd Barrett. It's strange that his name sounds the same as yours!\" [2693] \"His real name should be Roger Keith Barrett (Roger Keith Barrett). I guess he took the name Syd to imitate me.\" [2694] Looking at the two smiling faces, it's like they're joking back and forth. [2695] \"Today's encore is just this song.\" [2696] Zhizhu walked up to the combination sound system and quickly selected a CD. Since there was still a power outage, he put it into the portable CD player he had brought from downstairs. [2697] An odd piece of music began, without melody, only with strong, weak, high, and low tones. It was played on a piano, violin, flute, and cello quartet, with a female voice that could only be described as \"hair-raising.\" No, it was not a soprano! It was more like a screaming patient with severe mental illness. The eccentric, colorless modern music was unbearable for me, as it seemed to sing about nothingness and madness. The Beethoven piece earlier was so beautiful...\n[2698] \"This is Arnold Schoenberg's 'Pierrot lunaire' (Pierrot of the Moon), subtitled 'The Drunken Moon's Pierrot.' \" Zhido told us this, and then looked at Jiang Sheng, \"Do you know who the poem was written by that the music was composed for?\" [2699] Mr. Jiang nodded slightly, \"It's Albert Giraud. He's Belgian, isn't he?\" [2700] \"Haha...Yes, that's right! And this fellow here is Giro. EGAMI ZIRO (note: \"Giro\" is pronounced similarly to \"Ni-rou\" in Japanese.)?\" [2701] \"That's right! Even just for tonight, let's have a combination like this! Sid and Giraud!\" [2702] I had never heard of this poet's name before. [2703] \"What are you singing about?\" [2704] \"The poem is about the continuation of the fantasy of the moonlit night.\" Zhido listened intently, seeming to be in a good mood, \"Listen! Now, the flamboyant Pierrot is coming. Under the moonlight, the dark, sacred washbasin has the brilliance of crystal bottles and the sound of flowing water. He is on the washbasin, putting on makeup with the clear moonlight.\" [2705] \"What poem is this? It's very similar to Inoue Nobuko's style!\" [2706] \"Yes! The poem describes Pierrot drilling a hole in Cassandre's head, stuffing Turkish tobacco into a cherrywood pipe, and then smoking it. This is very similar to Inoue Jun'ichi's style, but I can't stand the name Inoue Jun'ichi. I thought he took the pronunciation of fireflies and reversed it to use 'Taruho' as his pen name, which I didn't like very much, but I recently found out that it's his real name.\" [2707] Zhu Du became verbose and began to explain the content of the German lyrics to us. The story is not particularly special. Under the moonlight, the comedians Kurenbo, his rival Cassandre, the fickle girl Kurenbo, the poet who loves her, and the elderly women who love Kurenbo all take the stage, and strange and decadent fantasies unfold. The play is not only strange and decadent, but also bloody. In the \"Chopin's Waltz\" section, to my surprise, the image of \"tuberculosis\" appears, which is an ominous sign; in the \"Madonna\" section, it describes the Madonna suffering from blood flowing endlessly from her eyes-shaped red wounds; and in the \"Theft\" section, it compares the noble ruby to a drop of blood to sing it. [2708] \"Mr. Zhu Du, I think this song is not suitable for the memorial night of Mr. Onizuka. The blood smell is too strong!\" [2709] Zhu Du intertwined his fingers intricately, listening, \"There was indeed a lot of blood. I chose this song because the painter also liked it. In addition, although this song ends with a farewell to the dead, it may not be completely unsuitable. At dawn, Pierrot sails back to his southern homeland with lotus leaves as a boat and moonlight as a rudder.\" [2710] I didn't say anything else. Although it's hard to tell what kind of emotions Zhu Du has for his deceased friend, he seems to be expressing his condolences in his own unique way. [2711] \"Who is that Sted Borette?\" I asked the headmaster. [2712] \"He was a rock musician in Pink Floyd (Pink Floyd, a British rock band formed in 1965, with a musical style leaning towards electronic and psychedelic rock. The original soul figure was Sted Borette, who left the band in 1968 due to mental illness and drug use.)\" [2713] \"I've heard of Pink Floyd, but is there such a person?\" [2714] \"He left quickly. He went crazy.\" [2715] I remembered the \"Golden Hair\" I had just heard. It was the music that came from the other side of the mirror... If Zhitu killed Onizuka...\n[2716] If so, Zi Du would be mocking the dead under the guise of a memorial service! [2717] I clenched my teeth, wanting to say casually, \"Zi Du, you seem to be ready to spit at fate at any time.\" [2718] It was late at night. [2719] On the other side of the broken bridge, the ones who probably slept were the ones who lived there! [2720] Chapter Nine: The Result of the Meeting - Kei\n[2721] 1\n[2722] \"It seems he's really dead,\" I bent down to look at the fallen Sagihara, asking the examining Wakayama. [2723] Sagihara's eyes were wide open, but his eyelids were motionless.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."